Synthesize the following 3 reviews into a meta-review:

- {"summary": "The paper introduces a theoretical framework for continual self-play in machine learning, focusing on the computational expressiveness of the learning process under explicit resource budgets. It formalizes a generator-executor-verifier-buffer loop for continual learning with self-proposed curricula, characterizes the expressiveness of the evolving loop in terms of SPACE complexity classes, and demonstrates monotone capability growth under specific exploration schedules and curriculum learnability mechanisms. The paper positions continual self-play as a principled framework for understanding data-free improvement under resource constraints, separating capability universality from alignment and safety.", "significance_and_novelty": "The paper's novelty lies in its process-level characterization of self-play training loops, linking them to classical SPACE complexity classes and providing finite-time completeness bounds. It shifts focus from architectural universality to learning-process universality, offering a reusable template for exploration schedules, retention policies, and context scaling.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["The paper provides a rigorous theoretical foundation for continual self-play, formalizing the computational expressiveness of the learning process under resource constraints.", "It introduces a novel Coverage-Persistence-Learning (CPL) decomposition, which isolates essential mechanisms for computational universality in self-play systems.", "The paper bridges architecture-level completeness and learning dynamics, mapping prompt length onto classical SPACE complexity hierarchies.", "It offers practical insights into the design of exploration schedules and buffer management, enhancing the applicability of theoretical findings."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["The paper relies on strong assumptions, such as unbounded replay buffers and fair sampling, which may limit its practical applicability.", "The exploration assumption, particularly the coverage oracle G_E, is idealized and may be challenging to implement in real-world systems.", "The paper does not address the global dynamical system formed by the coupled evolution of the generator, solver, and buffer, leaving open questions about gradient-based self-play tracking.", "The theoretical guarantees are capability results rather than efficiency guarantees, and the paper does not claim sample-efficient learning or polynomial-time convergence."], "suggestions": ["Consider extending the analysis to include finite-precision universality, addressing the impact of rounding noise on coverage guarantees.", "Explore the integration of interactive verification mechanisms, such as interactive proofs or human overseers, to enhance robustness in safety-sensitive settings.", "Investigate the practical implementation of the coverage oracle G_E, potentially through hybrid designs combining hand-crafted curricula and adaptive seeding.", "Develop a more refined analysis of buffer management, accounting for distributional shift, task interference, and catastrophic collapse in large-scale deployments."], "rating": 8, "confidence": 4, "soundness": 4, "presentation": 4, "contribution": 4}

- {"summary": "Theoretical analysis of computational power in self-play training loops, showing Turing-completeness and SPACE-class expressiveness under resource constraints.", "significance_and_novelty": "First theoretical framework linking self-play training loops to classical computational complexity classes, with novel Turing-completeness results for the learning process itself.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["The paper provides a novel theoretical framework for analyzing the computational power of self-play training loops, addressing an important gap in the literature (Section 1).", "The formal characterization of expressiveness via SPACE classes and Turing-completeness results are rigorous and well-supported (Section 6.1).", "The modular Coverage-Persistence-Learning (CPL) decomposition offers a reusable template for analyzing continual learning systems (Section 1.2).", "The resource-bounded analysis provides practical insights for real-world implementations (Section E.4)."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["The finite-time guarantees are exponential in horizon length, limiting practical applicability (Section 1.2).", "The analysis assumes sufficient capacity for per-task convergence without addressing catastrophic forgetting (Section E.3).", "The focus on expressiveness rather than efficiency leaves open important questions about learning speed (Section 1.2)."], "suggestions": ["Include empirical validation of the SPACE hierarchy predictions in a controlled setting.", "Discuss potential extensions to polynomial-time learning regimes.", "Add concrete examples of how the CPL template applies to existing self-play systems."], "rating": 8, "confidence": 4, "soundness": 4, "presentation": 3, "contribution": 4}

- {"summary": "The paper formalizes continual self-play as a computational process, analyzing its expressiveness and capability growth under explicit resource budgets. It introduces a generator-executor-verifier-buffer loop, characterizes its computational power via SPACE[\u00b7] classes, and separates capability universality from alignment/safety. Theoretical results are complemented by practical implementation insights.", "significance_and_novelty": "The paper provides a novel theoretical framework for continual self-play, formalizing its computational dynamics and separating capability universality from alignment/safety. This advances understanding of data-free improvement under resource constraints.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["The paper introduces a rigorous theoretical framework for continual self-play, formalizing its computational dynamics (Abstract).", "It provides a process-level characterization of expressiveness, linking the evolving loop to SPACE[\u00b7] classes (Abstract).", "The separation of capability universality from alignment/safety offers a principled approach to understanding self-play (Abstract).", "Practical implementation insights complement theoretical results, enhancing applicability (Appendix B)."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["The theoretical model assumes an abstract replay buffer with fair scheduling, which may not hold in real-world systems (G.2 Implementation Challenges).", "The paper lacks empirical validation of its theoretical claims, limiting practical impact (Abstract).", "The exploration guarantees rely on architectural constraints, which may be difficult to implement in practice (Remark 33).", "The computational power analysis does not address the discovery of programs via self-play, leaving a gap in understanding (2.2 Neural-Network Turing Completeness)."], "suggestions": ["Include empirical validation to support theoretical claims (Abstract).", "Extend the theoretical model to account for real-world replay buffer limitations (G.2 Implementation Challenges).", "Provide more detailed guidance on implementing exploration guarantees in practice (Appendix B).", "Address the discovery of programs via self-play to enhance understanding of computational power (2.2 Neural-Network Turing Completeness)."], "rating": 6, "confidence": 4, "soundness": 3, "presentation": 3, "contribution": 3}
